# Aegis - environment template
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in real values:  cp .env.example .env
# `.env` is gitignored; this file is the tracked contract and must never
# contain a real credential.
#
# Every variable below is read somewhere in src/ - the file reference on each
# one is where. If you add a new process.env lookup, add it here too, or the
# next person to clone this repo has no way to know it exists.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED - the app will not start or will fail closed without these
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Signs and verifies session cookies (HMAC). src/lib/session.ts throws on
# startup if this is unset - deliberately, since a default secret would let
# anyone forge an Admin session cookie. Also used as the NextAuth secret.
# Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
SESSION_SECRET=

# MongoDB connection string. src/lib/mongo.ts throws if unset.
# Local:  mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017
# Atlas:  mongodb+srv://<user>:<password>@<cluster>.mongodb.net
MONGODB_URI=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION - has a localhost fallback, so dev works without it
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Public origin of the deployment. Baked into verification, password-reset,
# and portfolio-report email links (src/app/api/auth/register/route.ts,
# forgot-password/route.ts). Falls back to http://localhost:4028, which means
# a production deploy that omits this silently emails users localhost links.
# No trailing slash.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:4028

# Canonical URL NextAuth uses to build Google OAuth callbacks. Read by the
# next-auth library itself rather than by our code, so it never appears in a
# grep of src/. Must match NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL and must be registered as an
# authorized redirect origin in the Google Cloud console.
NEXTAUTH_URL=http://localhost:4028

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE TWO APPLICATION SURFACES
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# One deployment serves two applications, separated by hostname:
#
#   APP_HOST    the trader application. /admin and /api/admin 404 here.
#   ADMIN_HOST  the operations console. Serves ONLY /admin and /api/admin.
#
# src/lib/admin/host.ts decides which surface a request is on, and every admin
# route handler and page re-checks it. In PRODUCTION, ADMIN_HOST must be set:
# without it the classifier falls back to matching any hostname beginning with
# "admin.", and the guards refuse to serve the console at all rather than rely
# on that. In development the fallback is what makes admin.localhost work with
# no DNS setup.
#
# Both accept a bare hostname or a full origin.
APP_HOST=app.example.com
ADMIN_HOST=admin.example.com

# Legacy alias for ADMIN_HOST, still read as a fallback. Prefer ADMIN_HOST.
ADMIN_APP_URL=http://admin.localhost:4028

# Serve the console at /admin on the ordinary app host instead of a subdomain.
#
# Leave this OFF wherever a subdomain is possible. The subdomain split is what
# makes /api/admin unreachable from the trader origin; turning this on gives
# that up and leaves requireAdmin (session + permission) as the only gate.
#
# It exists for deployments that cannot have the subdomain at all. The case
# that forced it: a Vercel project on its default *.vercel.app URL. Arbitrary
# subdomains of vercel.app cannot be registered, so admin.myproject.vercel.app
# will never resolve no matter what ADMIN_HOST says - without this flag such a
# deployment has no reachable console.
#
# Set it to exactly true, 1 or yes. Anything else - including the string
# "false" - leaves it off.
#
# When on, ADMIN_HOST is not required and is ignored.
# ADMIN_PATH_ROUTING=true

# Optional parent cookie domain for the TRADER session cookie, when the app
# spans subdomains - for example .example.com. Leave empty for host-only
# cookies.
#
# This does NOT affect the admin session cookie, deliberately. That one is
# always host-only and carries no Domain attribute, which is what keeps it
# from ever being sent to app.example.com. See src/lib/admin/cookie.ts.
SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADMIN CONSOLE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The one-time bootstrap that establishes the single MainAdmin. There is no
# path through the console to create one: if there were, compromising an admin
# session would be enough to mint permanent top-level authority.
#
#   npm run admin:bootstrap:dry-run    # see what it would do
#   npm run admin:bootstrap
#
# Safe to re-run. The account is created with mustChangePassword and mustSetPin
# set, so this password gets exactly one sign-in - remove these variables from
# the environment afterwards.
#
# Password rules: at least 14 characters, mixing three of lowercase,
# uppercase, digits, symbols.
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_EMAIL=
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_PASSWORD=
ADMIN_BOOTSTRAP_NAME=Main Administrator

# Admin session lifetimes, in minutes. Defaults are 8 hours absolute and 30
# minutes idle. The idle window slides on each request; the absolute one does
# not. Both are enforced server-side against the AdminSession row, so shrinking
# them takes effect on existing sessions immediately.
# ADMIN_SESSION_MAX_MINUTES=480
# ADMIN_SESSION_IDLE_MINUTES=30

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL - features degrade gracefully when these are absent
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Google OAuth (src/app/api/auth/[...nextauth]/route.ts). Without these the
# "Sign in with Google" button fails; email/password auth still works.
# Console: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Transactional email via Resend (src/lib/email.ts,
# src/app/api/portfolio-builder/route.ts). Both call sites check for the key
# and skip sending when it is missing, so signup still succeeds - the user
# just never receives a verification mail.
RESEND_API_KEY=
# Verified sender address. resend.dev is Resend's shared sandbox domain,
# usable for testing; production needs your own verified domain.
EMAIL_FROM=onboarding@resend.dev

# Mongo database name (src/lib/mongo.ts). Defaults to "aegis" when unset.
MONGODB_DB=aegis

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT CURRENTLY WIRED UP
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# CoinGecko demo key, for higher rate limits. The header that would send it is
# commented out at src/lib/coingecko.ts:38, so setting this today has no
# effect - all CoinGecko calls go out unauthenticated on the free tier
# (~50-100 req/min, mitigated by the 1-minute cache in that same file).
# Uncomment that line before relying on this.
# COINGECKO_API_KEY=

# Field-level encryption key for KYC identity numbers (32 bytes, base64).
# Generate: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# MUST differ per environment. Losing it makes stored ID numbers unrecoverable.
PII_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ============================================================================
# EXCHANGE / LIVE TRADING
# ============================================================================
# Which venue signal flows trade on, and whether they may move real money.
# Read by src/lib/exchange/config.ts. Nothing here is stored in the database
# and nothing is settable from the admin console: a "go live" button is one
# compromised session away from trading customer funds, an env var needs
# deploy access.
#
# EXCHANGE_MODE     paper (default) | binance
#   paper   Orders are simulated, but against REAL Binance prices, real lot
#           sizes and real minimum notionals. Needs no credentials. This is
#           not the old bot-pnl.ts random walk - a real strategy runs against
#           real market data and only the counterparty is imaginary.
#   binance Orders go to Binance. Defaults to TESTNET.
EXCHANGE_MODE=paper

# EXCHANGE_NETWORK  testnet (default) | mainnet
# Only meaningful when EXCHANGE_MODE=binance. Testnet credentials are free
# from https://testnet.binance.vision and trade fake money on real prices.
EXCHANGE_NETWORK=testnet

# Credentials. Required for EXCHANGE_MODE=binance on either network.
# Give the API key ONLY spot trading permission. Never enable withdrawals on
# it: the platform never needs to move funds off the exchange, so a key that
# can is a key that leaks that capability if the key leaks. Bind it to your
# server's IP if the venue supports it.
EXCHANGE_API_KEY=
EXCHANGE_API_SECRET=

# The third key. Mainnet requires EXCHANGE_MODE=binance AND
# EXCHANGE_NETWORK=mainnet AND this set to exactly true/1/yes. Two variables
# would be enough to describe the intent; the third exists because the first
# two are things someone sets while wiring up credentials, and neither reads
# as "start trading real money now".
#
# With any of the three missing the platform trades TESTNET and logs why.
# It never falls through to mainnet.
# EXCHANGE_ALLOW_LIVE_TRADING=true

# Paper cost model. Defaults match Binance spot taker (0.1%) so paper results
# are not quietly better than live ones. Rejected if negative or above 10%.
# EXCHANGE_PAPER_FEE_RATE=0.001
# EXCHANGE_PAPER_SLIPPAGE_RATE=0.0005

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Demo mode
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated performance figures, for showing the product before there is real
# trading history to show. OFF unless set to exactly true/1/yes.
#
# When on, the dashboard's P&L chart and flow cards display an invented curve
# with the win rate below, and every surface that renders those figures also
# renders a "Demo" banner saying they are not real trades. The label is not
# optional and is not configurable - this platform accepts deposits, and a
# generated profit figure reaching a depositor unlabelled is a claim about
# their money that cannot be honoured.
#
# Never set this in a production environment serving real customers.
# DEMO_MODE=true

# Share of days that close up, 0 to 1. Defaults to 0.7 (a 70/30 split).
# DEMO_WIN_RATE=0.7

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduler
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED for any trading to happen at all.
#
# Signal flows do not run on their own. A cron job calls /api/cron/trading on a
# schedule (every 5 minutes - see vercel.json), and that endpoint is what makes
# every running flow evaluate its strategy. Without this variable the endpoint
# refuses every call and flows sit at 0.0% forever, however healthy everything
# else looks.
#
# The endpoint fails closed: unset means refuse, not "open to everyone". An
# unauthenticated endpoint that drives trading is one guessed URL away from a
# stranger setting the platform's trading cadence.
#
# Generate one and set it in the deployment environment:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
#
# On Vercel this is all that is needed - Vercel Cron sends it automatically as
# a bearer token. Anywhere else, have the scheduler send:
#   Authorization: Bearer <CRON_SECRET>
CRON_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Trading is HALTED by default
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not an environment variable - a database one, and mentioned here because it
# is the other thing that stops a correctly configured platform from trading.
#
# TradingControl.tradingHalted defaults to true, and a missing document counts
# as halted too, so the safe state is the one you get by doing nothing. Lift it
# from the console at /admin/trading. Only the MainAdmin can; anyone granted
# trading.halt can put it back.
